String is immutable in Java. Methods like concat(), replace(), and toUpperCase() do not modify the existing String object. Instead, they create and return a new String object. In your example, str.concat("babu") creates a new String "Nagendrababu", but since the returned value is not assigned back to str, the original String "Nagendra" remains unchanged. To update the value, we must assign the result back: str = str.concat("babu");.


