String str = "Nagendra" creates a String literal and stores it in the String Constant Pool. If the same value already exists, Java reuses the existing object. String str1 = new String("Babu") always creates a new object in Heap Memory, and the literal "Babu" is also maintained in the String Constant Pool. SCP (String Constant Pool) is a special memory area used to store and reuse String literals, which improves memory efficiency and performance. Therefore, string literals are generally preferred over new String().




